# Advanced Build Options

The [Installation](install) guide covers the standard `make build`. This
chapter collects the non-standard build paths: building without the Node
toolchain, build customization, serving the web UI from disk during
development, and the local CI checks.

None of this is needed to install and run herold. Reach for it only when
the standard build does not fit your environment.

## Building without the Node toolchain

`make build` runs the pnpm workspace build to produce the web assets,
then compiles the binary. On a host with no Node or pnpm -- a Go-only CI
runner, for instance -- skip the web step with the `nofrontend` build
tag:

```bash
GOFLAGS="-tags nofrontend" make build-server
```

`make build-server` compiles only the Go binary; use it whenever the web
assets are already current and only Go code has changed. The
`nofrontend` tag swaps in a stub for the embedded web assets, so the
build succeeds with no Node in the environment. The resulting binary
serves a placeholder page in place of the Suite app -- suitable for
backend development and Go-only CI, not for production.

## Build customization

`make` exposes a few variables for non-standard environments:

| Variable   | Default | Purpose                                      |
|------------|---------|----------------------------------------------|
| `GO`       | `go`    | Path to the Go toolchain binary.             |
| `GOFLAGS`  | (empty) | Extra flags passed to every `go` invocation. |
| `FUZZTIME` | `30s`   | Per-target budget for `make fuzz-short`.     |

## Serving the web UI from disk

A release binary embeds the Suite app at compile time and serves it from
the public listener at `/`. During frontend development you can point a
running server at a freshly built dist tree instead, so a `pnpm build`
appears on the next browser refresh with no herold rebuild:

```toml
[server.suite]
asset_dir = "/abs/path/to/web/apps/suite/dist"
```

The override is read from disk on every request. The path is resolved at
server start -- relative paths are taken from the working directory, the
same convention as `data_dir` -- and the directory must contain
`index.html`, or the server refuses to start with a clear error.

## Local CI checks

The local CI lane mirrors what runs in continuous integration:

```bash
make ci-local
```

This runs the formatting checks, `go vet`, the test suite, and
`vulncheck`. `staticcheck` and the dual-backend integration tests are
separate targets (`make staticcheck`, `make interop-test`).
